---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const releases = [
  {
    version: '3.0.0',
    date: 'July 30, 2026',
    title: 'Everything about a feature lives in one folder',
    summary: 'v3 re-cuts gspec\'s specs along <strong>stable vs. growing</strong>. The style guide becomes a pure design system. Architecture becomes thin, high-level, and stops growing. Everything that <em>does</em> grow moves into <code>gspec/features/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> — PRD, architecture, a design you can open in a browser, and the task plan, side by side.<br /><br />The point is what an implementer has to read. Feature folders are deliberately <strong>denormalized</strong>: each one inlines the stack and architecture decisions it depends on, so building a feature means reading that folder and nothing else. The always-loaded context set stays near-constant as a project ages, and growth lands in folders that close when the feature ships.<br /><br />This release is the whole v3 line — the layout, the agents that write it, and everything a full autonomous dogfood build taught us about running it.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Feature folders.</strong> <code>gspec/features/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> holds <code>prd.md</code>, <code>arch.md</code>, <code>design.html</code> and <code>tasks.md</code>. Three writers fill it, each with its own validator gate, and the fan-out checkpoints per feature so an interrupted run resumes without regenerating finished work. The PRD stays profile-agnostic; its siblings are the opposite by design.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Designs are HTML you can actually open.</strong> Each feature\'s <code>design.html</code> is a self-contained mockup that renders from <code>file://</code> with no build step — so a human checks it in seconds, and it cannot quietly disagree with the tokens it claims to use. It copies the style guide\'s custom-property block and may not invent tokens of its own; a design that needs a new token is asking for a style-guide change.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Architecture stops growing.</strong> <code>gspec/architecture.md</code> carries the system tier — module boundaries, shared model at the name level, inter-module contracts — and each module gets a thin <code>&lt;dir&gt;/gspec/architecture.md</code> beside its code. Entity field lists, endpoint inventories and algorithms are now findings, not content: they belong to the feature that needs them. A single-module project keeps one file that plays both tiers.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Anchors, with one origin and many deltas.</strong> Every entity, endpoint, screen, component, rule and machine gets a rigid heading and a machine-readable status line. The first feature to define one is the origin; later features write <code>amends:</code> deltas stating only what changed. Verified on a real build: <strong>83 anchors, 7 shared across features, zero duplicate origins</strong> — the feature written last found every prior definition across five sibling folders.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>The style guide is tokens only.</strong> Palette, type scale, spacing, radius, elevation, themes, icons, a computed contrast table — the vector for implementation, never the concrete elements. Component specimens and imagery direction move to the feature designs that actually use them.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Deterministic floors run before any agent does.</strong> Anchor grammar, origin uniqueness, <code>amends:</code> targets resolving, screen coverage in both directions, <code>[P]</code> parallelism safety, <code>covers:</code> quotes appearing verbatim in the PRD, token literals outside the token block, stubs behind checked boxes, checked tasks whose files were never written. All pure text, all free, all fixed by a targeted revision instead of an agent run spent rediscovering them.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Per-agent model assignment and real token accounting.</strong> <code>.gspec/config.json</code> maps agents to models by name, role tier, or default, so the cheapest checkers stop running on the most expensive model. Every run now reports input, output, turns and cost per agent — which is how we learned the implementer is <strong>92% of a build\'s input</strong>, and that a stage gets cheaper by reading less, not by writing less. Each agent\'s runs are also split into <strong>authoring vs revision</strong>, reported as input-per-run for each: a per-agent total says who reads the most, but only the split says whether a targeted repair actually reads less than the run that wrote the thing.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>The build warns when your agents are older than the gspec running them.</strong> Agent, command and skill files are copies; upgrading never rewrites them. A ten-hour measured run executed entirely against day-old definitions — a QA bar fixed that morning failed the same stage seven times because the fix never reached the project, and nothing could notice. <code>install</code> now stamps its version and the build compares it. It warns rather than blocks, and a project installed before stamping is told the check could not run, because silence would read as "fresh".',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>A QA revision is a repair, not a rewrite — now including code.</strong> Every spec stage already sent a surgical revision naming only the findings; the implement stage re-sent the entire build prompt with a verdict stapled on, which is the shape that makes an agent generate more material instead of fixing what is there. Both implement loops now carry the findings alone, oldest first, and that history <strong>survives a resume</strong> — previously it lived in memory, so a resumed build handed the writer a blank slate and let it repeat a fix it had already been told did not work.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>Transient engine failures stop ending builds.</strong> A dropped connection is not a defect: a truncated validator response used to be graded as a failure and handed to the implementer as "API Error: Connection closed mid-response" to repair. An absent verdict now re-runs the <em>validator</em> — with the SAME escalating waits the implementer gets (30s / 120s / 270s), which for a long time were wired only into the implement loop. A validator that re-ran instantly and only once meant a momentary network fault took the whole build: one run died on <code>API Error: Unable to connect to API (ENOTFOUND)</code> at the plan stage with both attempts inside the same blip, and the specs behind it were fine. An outage is usually wider than one pause. A capacity limit still surfaces immediately — it resets on a clock, and retrying only spends another run on the same wall. That now includes <em>validators</em>, which never learned it: a real run died on <code>You&#39;ve hit your session limit · resets 12:30am</code>, having re-run the validator straight into the same wall and then reported &quot;no verdict twice — an engine error&quot; with advice to fix something that was not broken. A limit is now named as a limit, with the reset quoted and the instruction to wait rather than investigate.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>A resume skips work that is already done.</strong> A scope with every task checked no longer spends an implementer run discovering that — measured at roughly 50 minutes per feature on a run where all 88 tasks were complete. The gates still run against what is on disk; only the agent is skipped.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>Readers stopped rejecting correct answers.</strong> A run turned up a whole family of these: <code>VERDICT: **PASS**</code> read as no verdict, an unbulleted <code>covers:</code> matching none of 88 tasks, three legitimate wave-plan JSON shapes discarded, <code>{"waves": []}</code> — a correct "no work remains" — treated as gibberish, backticked API references like <code>jwt.encode</code> mistaken for missing files, paths named the way people name them (<code>(library)/layout.tsx</code>) reported absent, and a findings <em>table</em> whose revision reason came out as <code>| Severity | Finding | Fix |</code> — column names where the finding should be. Each now normalizes rather than dictates: pin the meaning, not the shape.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>Evidence is kept instead of summarized away.</strong> Four separate diagnostics pointed at nothing: an unreadable build plan said its output was "in the run log" where it had never been written, the implementation lint printed a count without the findings, and the reconcile audit — a stage whose <em>entire</em> output is a report — discarded it on success and kept it only when the agent crashed. Rejected plans now go to <code>.gspec/build/unparsed-plan.md</code>, audit findings to <code>.gspec/build/audit-report.md</code>, and lint findings are printed as they are fixed. The per-feature lint (feature arch, design, plan) kept the bare-count defect after the implement lint was fixed for it, and a dogfood run died there: the terminal violation survived, but the other finding in the same round was gone. It now prints every finding and records them to <code>qa-failures.md</code>, which claims to hold every failing verdict a run observes.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>A mechanical fix no longer trades one violation for another.</strong> Renaming an anchor is the single fix that can introduce a <em>different</em> violation, and the budget only covers one round. Observed end to end twice in one run: a writer qualified a shared anchor (<code>### Entity: SimEvent (wave variants)</code>) so several features could each extend one union, the grammar floor rejected the name, and the writer renamed it to the canonical <code>### Entity: SimEvent</code> — correct, and the only possible name — then wrote <code>defined-in:</code> because nothing in that finding said otherwise. The second origin failed the stage on re-lint with the retry already spent. The fix prompt now carries the rule the rename inherits: grep for the new heading first, and amend rather than re-originate if it already exists.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong><code>verify.sh</code> can no longer hang the build silently.</strong> A run sat about an hour on <code>docker info</code> against a daemon that was down, and a stalled script looks identical to a healthy one from outside. The driver now stops a script that produces no output for 10 minutes — idle, not a total cap, because provisioning a database and running integration tests legitimately takes many minutes — kills the whole process group so the hung grandchild goes with it, and reports it as an environment problem rather than a code failure. The engineer contract now requires every preflight to probe with a timeout and fail with a message instead of waiting.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>A memoized pass now expires when the checker changes.</strong> Content-addressed memoization skipped re-validating any deliverable whose file hash was unchanged — but it keyed on the deliverable alone, never on what judged it. So fixing a gate never re-examined anything that had already passed: the build kept running yesterday\'s <em>verdicts</em>, the same disease install-version stamping fixed one layer up when a run was caught executing yesterday\'s <em>agents</em>. Caught the moment it mattered: correcting the inverted <code>[P]</code> floor mid-run left the one feature carrying a real conflict marked &quot;unchanged since it passed&quot;, so the corrected check never saw the file it was written for. Memos now record the gspec version beside the hash, and an upgrade invalidates them — the conservative direction, since a redundant re-validation costs one cheap run and a skipped one ships the defect.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The <code>[P]</code> parallelism check was inverted.</strong> It flagged a <code>[P]</code> task for depending on a <em>sequential</em> one and let a <code>[P]</code>-on-<code>[P]</code> dependency through — backwards on both halves. The hazard is between siblings: <code>[P]</code> says these run alongside each other, so one depending on another is the thing that cannot work. A sequential dependency is a <em>barrier</em>, which is the normal shape — one foundation task, then a fan-out. The old reading also made the marker unusable: a plan is written before any work exists, so no task with a dependency could ever carry <code>[P]</code>, while the decomposer is required to produce honest ones and report how many. And it made a static verdict move with execution — the same file failed before its foundation task was checked and passed after. Measured on a first-draft plan: <strong>11 findings across 3 of 8 features, every one wrong</strong>, while a fourth feature passed carrying a real <code>[P]</code>-on-<code>[P]</code> conflict. Corrected, the same plans yield zero false findings and four true ones. Its unit tests had encoded the inverted rule, including one asserting that two <code>[P]</code> tasks depending on each other &quot;start together&quot;.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>Three checks that were verifying nothing.</strong> A bullet-only pattern matched none of 88 real tasks; splitting <code>covers:</code> on <code>;</code> missed the several-quotes-per-line form every real plan uses, so the check that catches a capability ticked ahead of its plan reported clean on exactly the input it exists for; and a scripted install exited 0 having skipped hooks and preamble. A check that matches nothing is worse than one that is wrong, because it is silent.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>A run records what stopped it.</strong> On a long build a usage limit is routine — it resets on a clock and the run is continued hours later — but nothing kept a structured trace of that: <code>last-failure.md</code> is overwritten by the next failure and removed when the build completes, and a stage that eventually passes shows only <code>attempts: 2</code> with no reason. A finished run could not say whether it had been interrupted at all. <code>run.json</code> now carries a <code>pauses</code> list — stage, when, why, whether it was a usage limit, and how long the wait was — the resume prints what it is continuing, and the closing report adds an <strong>Interruptions</strong> section. Stage time deliberately excludes waiting, so this is what explains a run that took thirteen hours of wall clock and three of stage time.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>A fan-out says how far along it is.</strong> Stages that write one file per feature printed their header and then nothing until the whole stage finished — 29 minutes of silence on a real run, which is exactly what a <em>stalled</em> stage looks like too, so the log could not tell them apart. Every per-feature stage now counts its work <code>[3/8]</code> through both halves: the concurrent writing phase (counting completions, since starts finish out of order) and the serial validation phase, which reported nothing at all before. The target is announced <em>before</em> it is worked on, so on a stage that stops progressing the last line printed is the feature it stopped on. A single-feature run stays uncounted — <code>[1/1]</code> is noise.<br /><br />Implementation counts too, and needed it most: the longest and most expensive stage in a build printed nothing between its wave plan and its gates. It now announces each wave with the scopes it is starting — so a stall names what is in flight — and counts each scope as it completes. Competitive research counts each competitor as it returns.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong><code>--dry-run</code> is a preview again.</strong> It used to persist the full run record — <code>run.json</code> with every stage marked done, plus <code>audit-report.md</code> and <code>unparsed-plan.md</code>. So previewing a plan and then building for real was refused with "a build run already exists", and following that message\'s own advice (<code>--resume</code>) resumed the imaginary finished run: every stage already done, nothing built, exit 0. The intent was there but written at each call site, and six of them never got it — so the rule now lives at the write boundary instead, where a new writer inherits it. The tests that should have caught this were seeding themselves from the very file that should not have existed.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>/gspec-migrate</code> to move an existing project to the v3 layout — it relocates files, renames <code>deployable:</code> to <code>module:</code> and stamps the spec version, then tells you to re-run <code>/gspec-architect</code> and <code>/gspec-plan</code> so the new tiers are written by the agents that own them. It never touches <code>style.html</code>/<code>style.md</code> or <code>gspec/design/</code>, because stripping them is unrecoverable; move those mockups into the relevant feature\'s <code>design.html</code> yourself. Then run <code>npx gspec -t &lt;target&gt;</code> once to refresh installed agents and record the version stamp — until you do, the build will note that agent freshness cannot be checked.',
  },
  {
    version: '2.7.0',
    date: 'July 27, 2026',
    title: 'Specs sized to the product — and a build that can no longer end in silence',
    summary: 'A full autonomous build of a single-stage browser game produced <strong>807 KB of specification</strong> — a 65 KB feature PRD longer than the architecture document, and a 175 KB style guide for a game that did not exist yet — and then died repeatedly while agents tried to read it. Nothing in gspec bounded how much specification got written: writers were told what a good spec contains and never how big one should be, so they optimized the only axis they were graded on, and the validators graded <em>that</em> on precision, which rewards more text. This release bounds it from both ends. Feature PRDs get a <strong>section contract</strong> that says what each section must <em>not</em> hold and where that content belongs instead; the foundation specs get proportionality rules; every deliverable gets a <strong>size budget</strong> scaled by a new <code>--scope</code> tier; and the driver now measures each spec as it lands and reports it against its budget. Going over is advisory — it is never a gate.<br /><br />The same release closes a second failure of the long unattended run: <strong>it could stop without telling anyone</strong>. A completed build and a build paused for spec review both exited <code>0</code>, so nothing watching a detached run could tell success from a pause; a writer that exited <code>0</code> having written nothing was waved through, and the <em>validator</em> got blamed for a file nobody had produced; and a run that was killed left no mark at all — the manifest stuck at <code>running</code>, no failure report, a log that simply stopped. Now every ending has its own exit code and its own record in <code>.gspec/build/status.json</code>, <code>gspec build --status</code> answers "how did it go?" in one line, and a crash writes its own epitaph.',
    changes: [
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>A distinct exit code for every ending, and <code>gspec build --status</code>.</strong> A run now exits <code>0</code> complete, <code>1</code> failed a gate, <code>2</code> paused for spec review, or <code>3</code> crashed — so a watcher branches on a number instead of reading prose. The matching state is written to <code>.gspec/build/status.json</code> at every transition (state, stage, reason, exit code, engine, and the driving pid), and <code>gspec build --status</code> prints it in one line and exits with that code. <code>run.json</code> is still the control state a resume works from; this is the answer to "how did it end?".',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>A crash writes its own epitaph.</strong> Handlers for <code>uncaughtException</code>, <code>unhandledRejection</code>, <code>SIGINT</code>, and <code>SIGTERM</code> record the ending <em>synchronously</em> (<code>process.exit</code> will not wait on a promise) and write <code>last-failure.md</code> before dying, so a killed run stops looking identical to one still working. They install only <em>after</em> intake, so a Ctrl+C aimed at the interview behaves as it always has, and never for <code>SIGHUP</code> — which would have defeated <code>nohup</code>. A <code>SIGKILL</code> outruns any handler, so <code>--status</code> treats a <code>running</code> record whose pid is gone as a crash, records that, and exits <code>3</code>.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>Your saved templates now reach an autonomous build.</strong> <code>gspec-templates</code> lets you keep reusable specs under <code>~/.gspec/{stacks,styles,practices,features}/</code>, and the four matching writers preload the skill — but the convention resolves <code>~</code> in the orchestrating <em>command</em>, which has a shell. An autonomous <code>gspec build</code> has no such command, and the writers have no <code>Bash</code>, so they were pointed at a folder they had no way to name: every saved template was ignored, <em>silently</em>, and indistinguishably from having none. The driver now resolves the library itself and lists the matching folder\'s candidates — absolute path, name, and description — in that stage\'s brief, printing what it found (<code>templates: practices 1 · stacks 2</code>). Which template to adopt is still the writer\'s judgment, and adapting it to the brief is still required. A stage is only ever offered its own type, and only its writer is (a planner decides scope, not content); <code>profile.md</code> and <code>architecture.md</code> still have no library, being inherently project-specific.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>A writer that exits 0 having written nothing now fails, naming itself.</strong> An engine that hits an auth failure, a rate limit, or a usage cap in headless mode reports it as its final message and exits clean. The driver took that as success: the stage marched on and the <em>validator</em> failed against a document nobody had written, pausing the run with the wrong culprit. Delivery — not the exit code — now decides whether a writer succeeded; it is retried once, then fails with the real reason. The existing "a crash after a good write is not a gate failure" recovery is unchanged. The same insistence covers the driver-written deliverables: the research synthesis must land on disk, and a plan-decomposer\'s output must actually contain task checkboxes before it is persisted as a plan. A validator that returns no <code>VERDICT:</code> line is now called out as unvalidated rather than counted as a quiet FAIL.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>A QA revision now knows which rule wins.</strong> Two rules from this release could point in opposite directions with nothing ranking them: "a revision that grows the document is a defect" (and, when the draft was already over budget, the flat "it must not grow at all") against a blocker finding like <em>eight required sections are missing</em> — a fix that multiplies the document\'s length. Which rule gave way was left to the writer\'s judgment. The revision prompt and the shared budget contract now state the precedence, ahead of the rule it governs: <strong>resolving a blocker or major finding outranks every size rule.</strong> Content a finding names as missing gets added in full, however far past the ceiling it takes the spec, and is never watered down to stay near a word count — an unresolved blocker fails the gate, an overage cannot. Growth is still only for what the findings require: everything else is resolved by replacing text, an over-budget draft pays for required additions by cutting restatement elsewhere, and an overage that survives is reported in the summary (with a decomposition recommendation for a feature or architecture spec) instead of being traded for an unresolved finding.',
      },
      {
        type: 'fixed',
        description: '<strong>The style stage could never pass its delivery check.</strong> A stage\'s declared outputs are <em>alternatives</em> — the style writer produces <code>gspec/style.html</code> <strong>or</strong> <code>gspec/style.md</code>, one format, and the validator target names the pair with "or". The new delivery check read them as a checklist and demanded both, so "did the writer deliver?" was permanently false for that stage: a perfectly good style guide was reported as "produced no deliverable", retried once, and then failed the run — and the companion recovery (accept an artifact that is present and well-formed even though the writer exited non-zero) could never fire either. Any one of a stage\'s outputs, present and non-empty, now counts as delivery.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>The <code>/gspec-build</code> command must report how the run ended.</strong> The command used to offer a harness-tracked background task and a detached <code>nohup</code> launch as equal options, and monitoring happened "after each user check-in" — so a detached run that failed an hour later reached nobody. It now prefers the tracked background task explicitly (that is the only launch where the harness wakes the agent when the process exits), says plainly when it cannot watch the run, and requires running <code>gspec build --status</code> and reporting the result the moment the run ends — including, especially, when it failed.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Section contract for feature PRDs.</strong> Each PRD section now states what it holds, what it must <em>not</em>, and where the excluded content belongs — capabilities carry observable acceptance criteria, not state machines, layout tables, or timing formulas (those are the architecture spec\'s job); Dependencies names siblings rather than restating them; Implementation Context is a fixed portability note and nothing else. Two rules do most of the work: a PRD writer <strong>does not read</strong> <code>profile.md</code>, <code>style.*</code>, <code>stack.md</code>, <code>practices.md</code>, or <code>architecture.md</code> (sibling PRDs are the only spec it reads, cross-linked by slug), and the required-section list is exhaustive — no invented "Implementation Details" or "Technical Architecture" sections. An optional <code>Deferred Decisions</code> section is now explicitly allowed, resolving a contradiction where the quality bar referred to a section the required list omitted.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>Size budgets, advisory by design.</strong> A new "Size budgets" table in the shared conventions skill gives every deliverable a word ceiling — 2,000 for the profile/stack/practices/style guides, 1,800 per feature PRD, 3,000 for the architecture — read by writers <em>and</em> validators, so the checker no longer pushes for detail the ceiling forbids. Counting includes tables (where an over-long spec usually hides) and exempts frontmatter and the practices <code>Enforcement</code> block. A budget is a ceiling, not a target: brevity is never a finding, a missing required section is still a defect, and <strong>no proposed fix may grow a spec already at its budget</strong> — revisions resolve findings by replacing text, not appending to it.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong><code>--scope small | standard | large</code>.</strong> The intake now asks how big the product is and records the tier in the brief; the flag overrides it, and it is pinned on the run manifest. The tier scales every budget by ×0.6 / ×1 / ×1.5, so a one-level game and a multi-deployable platform no longer get the same specification volume. Honored at resume time like <code>--qa-retries</code>, so a run whose specs came out too big can be retuned and continued rather than restarted.',
      },
      {
        type: 'added',
        description: '<strong>The driver measures what was actually written.</strong> After each writer runs, the build counts the produced spec and prints it against its budget (<code>gspec/features/x.md — 4,877 words, 2.7× its 1,800-word budget</code>), and feeds the overage into the next revision prompt. Measurement is in the driver because a model asked to self-limit reports compliance while writing lines twice as long — in the run that prompted this release, one spec was 8% <em>shorter</em> in lines than a known-good equivalent while being 60% larger. <strong>Over budget never fails a stage</strong>: it is logged, and QA records it as a <code>[minor]</code> finding that the severity contract keeps non-blocking.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>Proportionality rules for the foundation specs.</strong> Unlike the PRD case, the stack and practices boundary rules were intact and honored — those specs grew because several quality-bar items scale with item count and had no threshold. So: rationale is for <em>major</em> choices (a minor or forced choice gets a clause, not a table row of its own); "Not Applicable" means one line and a reason, not a section that declares itself N/A and then specifies itself anyway; don\'t enumerate what a lockfile or a formatter config already declares; and scale depth to the scope tier. The practices guide\'s CI/CD pipeline structure now has a declared home (under Version Control &amp; Collaboration) instead of writers inventing a section for it, and the style guide gets a one-specimen-per-pattern rule.',
      },
      {
        type: 'changed',
        description: '<strong>QA checks tier bleed and size.</strong> The QA persona\'s "Boundary violations" failure mode now explicitly covers a section that has started specifying <em>how</em> the system works inside a spec whose job is <em>what</em> — cite the block, name the spec it belongs to, and propose relocation rather than deletion. A new "Over budget" failure mode reports size but is capped at <code>[minor]</code> however large the overage. And every proposed fix must fit the budget: "add a section explaining…" is almost always the wrong fix.',
      },
    ],
    migration: 'Run <code>npx gspec</code> to install the updated skills and agents. Existing specs stay valid — this defines and bounds sections rather than removing them, so <code>spec-version</code> is unchanged and no <code>/gspec-migrate</code> run is needed. Nothing fails for being over budget; the first thing you will notice is size lines in the build log and occasional <code>[minor]</code> size notes in <code>.gspec/build/qa-failures.md</code>. If your product is smaller or larger than average, pass <code>--scope</code> (or let the intake record it) so the budgets scale with it. Runs started before 2.7.0 resume normally and default to the <code>standard</code> tier.<br /><br /><strong>One breaking change, and it is deliberate:</strong> the spec-review pause now exits <code>2</code> instead of <code>0</code>. Anything that treated <code>gspec build</code> exiting <code>0</code> as "the build finished" was silently wrong before — that was the whole bug — but a script or CI job with <code>if gspec build …; then deploy</code> will now take the failure branch on a pause. Update it to branch on the code: <code>0</code> complete, <code>1</code> failed, <code>2</code> paused for review, <code>3</code> crashed. A run started before 2.7.0 has no <code>status.json</code>; <code>gspec build --status</code> derives its state from the manifest instead, so a resume works either way.',
  },
  {
    version: '2.6.0',
    date: 'July 25, 2026',
    title: 'Per-agent model assignment — run each stage on the right-sized model',
